    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study has been carried out in two related parts. In the first part, analytical equations were derived which describe the pressure buildup and falloff behaviors in reservoirs due to the injection of water into wells.The line source injection solution (solved using the Boltzmann similarity transformation) and the injection solution for a multicomposjte system with a finite radius well were compared with the corresponding numerical results. The comparison study showed that the exact expression of the inner boundary is very important in two-phase problems as the pressure strongly depends on the location of front. As a result, the falloff and multirate injection solutions were developed based on the multicomposite system only.The first part also included the development of an improved deconvolution technique with new constraints and an improved nonlinear regression algorithm based on the Cholesky factorization, both of which were improved to deal with difficulties found in the pressure transient analysis in water injection wells.In the second part, the effect of water and oil relative permeabilities on pressure transient testing in water injection wells was investigated using the mathematical models developed in the first part. The investigation showed that the characteristics of relative permeability are reflected distinctly in the pressure and pressure derivative responses in the transition part between the early and late time ranges. After the investigation, the inverse problem of estimation of water and oil relative permeabilities from pressure transient data was discussed using the developed system, which consisted of the multicomposite model and the improved nonlinear regression algorithm. The study showed that the Corey permeability curves (one-shape factor model) can be determined uniquely from injection pressure data. It was possible to determine the two-exponent power law or Chierici (four shape factor model) relative permeability curves uniquely from pressure falloff data only. Interpretation results of field examples supported the practicality of the developed system.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
